= Solution
{parent=3}

The space is the <mapping torus> of the <antipodal map> $A:S^n\to S^n$. For $n\geq1$, its <mapping degree> is $\delta=(-1)^{n+1}$: $A$ is the restriction of $-I$ on $\mathbb R^{n+1}$, whose determinant is $(-1)^{n+1}$. The <Wang sequence> gives
$$
0\longrightarrow\operatorname{coker}(1-A_*:H_j(S^n)\to H_j(S^n))\longrightarrow H_j(T_A)\longrightarrow\ker(1-A_*:H_{j-1}(S^n)\to H_{j-1}(S^n))\longrightarrow0.
$$
The kernel terms are free abelian, so these short exact sequences split as groups. On $H_0(S^n)$ the map is the identity, and on $H_n(S^n)$ it is multiplication by $\delta$. For $n\geq2$ this gives
$$
\boxed{H_j(T_A;\mathbb Z)=\begin{cases}
\mathbb Z,&j=0,1,\\
\mathbb Z,&n\text{ odd and }j=n,n+1,\\
\mathbb Z/2,&n\text{ even and }j=n,\\
0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}}
$$
At $n=1$, both contributions to degree one are present, giving $H_0=H_2=\mathbb Z$ and $H_1=\mathbb Z^2$, with all other groups zero. At $n=0$, the antipodal map exchanges the two points of $S^0$, and the two cylinder intervals join into a single circle; thus $H_0=H_1=\mathbb Z$ and higher groups vanish. These are the <homology of the antipodal sphere mapping torus> cases.

= Solution
{parent=Paper 16/1}

For the triangle quotient, the <universal coefficient theorem for homology> gives $H_j(X;\mathbb F_2)=0$ for every $j>0$, because its only positive integral <homology> is $\mathbb Z/3$. A connected positive-dimensional <closed manifold> has a nonzero top mod-two <fundamental class>, independently of orientability. Thus this quotient cannot be <homotopy equivalent> to such a <manifold>. A connected zero-dimensional <closed manifold> is a point, which its nonzero $H_1(X;\mathbb Z)$ also excludes.

For the attached-band space, the same <universal coefficient theorem for homology> gives nonzero $H_2(X;\mathbb F_2)$ and zero homology above degree two. A connected <closed manifold> homotopy equivalent to it would consequently have dimension two. Its <Euler characteristic> would be one. The <classification theorem for surfaces> says that a closed connected surface of Euler characteristic one is the <real projective plane>. But its integral $H_1$ is $\mathbb Z/2$, differing from both possibilities computed in part 2. Thus this space is never <homotopy equivalent> to a <closed manifold>.

The antipodal <mapping torus> is already a <closed manifold>: gluing the two boundary components of $S^n\times[0,1]$ by a diffeomorphism gives a smooth $(n+1)$-manifold without boundary. For $n\geq1$, it is orientable exactly when $(-1)^{n+1}=1$, namely when $n$ is odd. In that case $-I$ on $\mathbb R^{n+1}$ is isotopic to the identity through rotations, so the <mapping torus> is diffeomorphic to $S^n\times S^1$. The $n=0$ case is a circle as above. **Only the third family can be homotopy equivalent to a closed manifold.**

