= Solution
{parent=4}

Take the **<Hopf map> $\eta:S^3\to\mathbb{CP}^1\cong S^2$**, given by $\eta(z_0,z_1)=[z_0:z_1]$. The domain has positive-dimensional <homology> only in degree three, while $H_3(S^2;\mathbb Z)=0$. In degree two the source group is zero, and all remaining positive-degree source groups are zero too. Hence the induced map on <homology> is zero in every positive degree.

For nontriviality, use the <Hopf fibration> $S^1\to S^3\xrightarrow{\eta}S^2$. It is a <fiber bundle>: phase multiplication gives each fibre, and choosing a normalized representative on either standard projective chart gives a local trivialization. The theorem invoked is the <long exact sequence of homotopy groups of a fibration>. Since the universal cover of $S^1$ is contractible, $\pi_2(S^1)=\pi_3(S^1)=0$. Its exact sequence consequently gives an isomorphism
$$
\eta_*:\pi_3(S^3)\longrightarrow\pi_3(S^2).
$$
The identity class of $S^3$ is nonzero, since an identity map cannot be null-homotopic while inducing the identity on $H_3(S^3;\mathbb Z)$. Its image under $\eta_*$ is the class of $\eta$, so that class is nonzero. **The Hopf map is not homotopic to a constant despite inducing zero on all positive-degree homology.**

